For either the conditional tagged vector or the signed-vector evolution just defined, put $$\xi_t=f_{t+1}-P_{i_t}f_t,
 \qquad
 w_t=\mathbb E\sum_{x\in V}f_t(x)^2\mathbf 1_{\{x^{i_t}\notin V_t\}}.$$ Then $\xi_t$ is conditionally centered given the past, and $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E\|\xi_t\|_2^2&=\tfrac12w_t,\label{noise:noise-energy}\\
 a_t&=\sum_{j=1}^{d}2^{-j}w_{t-j}\quad(t\ge d),
 \label{noise:memory-equality}\\
 a_{t+1}&=\tfrac12(a_t+w_t-2^{-d}w_{t-d})
             \le\tfrac12(a_t+w_t)\quad(t\ge d).
 \label{noise:one-step-memory}
\end{aligned}$$ Moreover, if $C_t=\mathbb E[f_tf_t^{\mathsf T}]$ and $e_x$ is the unit vector at $x$, then the exact covariance recursion is $$\begin{aligned}
 C_{t+1}&=P_{i_t}C_tP_{i_t}\notag\\
 &\quad+\frac14\sum_{\{x,z\}:z=x^{i_t}}
  \mathbb E\!\left[\mathbf 1_{\{|\{x,z\}\cap V_t|=1\}}
                     (f_t(x)+f_t(z))^2\right]
     (e_x-e_z)(e_x-e_z)^{\mathsf T},
 \label{noise:covariance}
\end{aligned}$$ where the last sum is over unordered pairs.

Fix $s\ge d-1$, put $a=s-d+1$, and let $H_0,H_1$ be the two halves defined by coordinate $i_s$. Conditional on $\mathcal F_a$, for $h\in\{0,1\}$, $$\label{mart:half-factorization}
 \mathbb E\!\left[
  \sum_{x\in H_h}f_s(x)^2\mathbf1_{\{x^{(i_s)}\in V_s\}}
       \,\middle|\,\mathcal F_a\right]
 =\frac{|V_a\cap H_{1-h}|}{n/2}
   \mathbb E\!\left[\sum_{x\in H_h}f_s(x)^2
       \,\middle|\,\mathcal F_a\right].$$ If both half densities at time $a$ are at least $\beta$ except on an $\mathcal F_a$-measurable event of probability at most $\eta$, then $$\label{mart:noise-bound}
 w_s\le(1-\beta)a_s+\eta.$$

Fix $0<\varepsilon\le1$. Start a sweep from deterministic $(F,f)$ with $|F|\ge\varepsilon n$, $\sum_xf(x)=0$, and energy $E_0=\|f\|_2^2$. Denote its output by $(F^*,f^*)$ and write $S_B=\sum_{x\in B}f^*(x)$ and $m_B=|F^*\cap B|$. For $B\in\mathcal B_j$, of size $s=2^j$, $$\label{sweeps:block-variance}
 \mathbb ES_B^2\le(s/n)E_0,\qquad
 \Pr(m_B<\varepsilon s/2)\le e^{-\varepsilon^2s/2}.$$ The second bound can alternatively be replaced by $\exp[-(1/2-e^{-1})\varepsilon s]$.

Start a sweep deterministically. Suppose two sibling blocks have size $s$, available counts $m_0,m_1$ and signed sums $S_0,S_1$. Let $\overline E_0,\overline E_1$ be their expected energies after their internal coordinates have been updated. The expected energy after their merging coordinate is $$\label{sweeps:merge-eq}
 (1-m_1/(2s))\overline E_0+
 (1-m_0/(2s))\overline E_1+S_0S_1/s.$$

Fix $p\in(0,1)$ and set $r=\lfloor d/2\rfloor$. For arbitrary $|F|\ge pn$ and zero-sum $f$, two sweeps satisfy $$\label{cycles:merge-factor}
 \mathbb E\|f_{2d}\|_2^2\le
 \left((1-p/4)^{d-r}+2^{-r}
             +2^{d-r}e^{-p2^r/8}\right)\|f_0\|_2^2.$$ In particular the factor is at most $n^{-\gamma_p}$ eventually, where $\gamma_p=-\frac14\log_2(1-p/4)>0$. It is also at most $n^{-p/16}$ eventually.

For every $t\ge0$, $$\label{cycles:autocorrelation-update}
 v_{t+1}=v_tL_{h(t)}+\frac12b_t(\delta_0-\delta_{e_{h(t)}}).$$ Consequently, for $t\ge d$, $$\label{cycles:memory-equation}
 r_t=\sum_{\ell=1}^d2^{-\ell}b_{t-\ell}.$$

Put $\theta=15/16$ and $\epsilon_n=2e^{-n/256}$. For $s\ge d$, $$\label{cycles:blocked-bound}
 b_s\le\theta r_s+\epsilon_n.$$ With $\gamma=63/64$, it follows that $$\label{cycles:autocorrelation-energy}
 r_t\le\gamma^{t-2d}+\frac{\epsilon_n}{1-\theta}
 \quad(t\ge2d),\qquad
 r_{1026d}\le2n^{-16}$$ for all sufficiently large $d$.

Fix $0<\varepsilon\le1$ and put $h=\lfloor d/2\rfloor$, $\sigma=1-\varepsilon/4$. For deterministic zero-sum signed mass supported on at least $\varepsilon n$ available positions, two sweeps satisfy $$\label{sweeps:two-sweep-explicit}
 \mathbb E E_{2d}\le E_0\left[
 \sigma^{d-h}+2^{-h}+(d+1)n e^{-\varepsilon^2 2^h/2}\right].$$ In particular, for every $0<b<\min\{-\tfrac12\log_2(1-\varepsilon/4),\tfrac12\}$, $\mathbb E E_{2d}\le n^{-b}E_0$ for all sufficiently large $d$. For $\varepsilon=1/16$, one may take $b=1/200$.

For deterministic $(H,x)$ at the start of a sweep, $$\begin{aligned}
\label{sweeps:collision-eq}
 \mathbb E E_d={}&\sum_{u\in H}s_H(\{u\})x(u)^2\\
 &+\sum_{i=1}^d2^{1-i}\sum_{B\in\mathcal B_i}
          s_H(B)x(B_0)x(B_1),\nonumber
\end{aligned}$$ where $x(A)=\sum_{u\in A}x(u)$ and $B_0,B_1$ are the children of $B$.

Choose the initial ordered card list uniformly and independently of all switches. Fix an exposure index with $m\ge\varepsilon n$ available positions, where $\varepsilon=1/q$ and $q$ is a fixed power of two. Put $b=\lfloor d/2\rfloor$ and $$\Delta_n=2n(n+1)\exp[-\varepsilon(1/2-e^{-1})2^b].$$ At sweep boundaries $t$, the tag process obeys $$\label{sweeps:averaged-recursion}
 \mathbb E E_{t+2d}\le\Delta_n+
 \left[(1+b/2)(1-\varepsilon/4)^{d-b}+2^{-b}\right]\mathbb E E_t.$$ With $q=128$, $R=128q$, and $T=2Rd$, $\mathbb E E_T\le n^{-8}+R\Delta_n$. Consequently the average endpoint TV distance of an ordered list of $n-n/q$ cards tends to zero.

For the tagged-card difference $x_t=p_t-m^{-1}{\bf1}_{H_t}$ with a fixed initial hidden set of size $m\ge\varepsilon n$, so that $E_0\le1$, put $a_t=\mathbb E E_t$ and $I_t=\sum_u x_t(u)^2{\bf1}_{\{u\oplus e_{i_t}\notin H_t\}}$. Then $$\label{sweeps:noise-identity}
 a_t=\sum_{j=1}^d2^{-j}\mathbb E I_{t-j}\quad(t\ge d).$$ For $t\ge d$, each of the following choices gives $\mathbb E I_t\le\gamma a_t+B_n$: $$\begin{array}{c|c|c}
 &\gamma&B_n\\ \hline
 \text{available count}&1-\varepsilon/2&2e^{-\varepsilon n/8}\\
 \text{blocker count}&1-\varepsilon/2&2e^{-\varepsilon^2n/4}.
 \end{array}$$ For either choice, every $1/2<\beta<1$ with $2\beta-1\ge\gamma$ satisfies $$\label{sweeps:refresh-bound}
 a_t\le\beta^{t-2d}+B_n/(1-\gamma)\qquad(t\ge0).$$

For every non-singleton coordinate block, $$\label{prefix:recursion}
 W_C=(1-\alpha_{C_1}/2)W_{C_0}
     +(1-\alpha_{C_0}/2)W_{C_1}+(1-\alpha_C)D_C.$$ If the total signed sum is zero, the expected energy after one sweep, denoted $\Phi(H,f)$, is therefore $$\label{prefix:expanded}
 \Phi(H,f)=\sum_{C\text{ non-singleton}}(1-\alpha_C)D_C
  \prod_{A\supsetneq C}
    \left(1-\frac{\alpha_{\operatorname{sib}_A(C)}}2\right).$$ Here the product runs over the proper coordinate-block ancestors of $C$, and $\operatorname{sib}_A(C)$ is the child of $A$ not containing $C$. Empty products equal one, and $$\label{prefix:telescoping}
 \sum_{C\text{ non-singleton}}D_C=\mathcal E(H,f).$$

Fix deterministic zero-sum data $(H,f)$ with $|H|\ge cn$, where $c=1/4$, and put $s=2^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}$. For uniform $g\in\mathcal A_d$, with probability at least $1-2n e^{-cs/8}$, every coordinate block of size at least $s$ has a fraction at least $c/2$ of its positions in $gH$. For every block $C$ of size $s$, $$\label{prefix:block-second-moment}
 \mathbb E_g S_C(gf)=0,\qquad
 \mathbb E_g S_C(gf)^2\le(s/n)\mathcal E(H,f).$$

For every integer $b\ge1$, every deterministic starting deck, and any ordered list of $k\le3n/4$ distinct labels, the endpoint law $\mu_{b,k}$ after $b$ sweeps satisfies, for sufficiently large $d$, $$\label{prefix:entropy-bound}
 D(\mu_{b,k}\|U_{\mathrm{inj},k})
          \le n^2 n^{-(b-1)/64}.$$ In particular at $b=769$, every such list has total-variation error at most $n^{-5}$.

For every fixed $0<\delta<1$ there is an integer $R=R(\delta)$ such that, for a uniform ordered $k$-tuple $X$ of distinct inputs with $k\le(1-\delta)n$, its image law $\mu_X$ after $Rd$ shuffles satisfies $$\mathbb E_X D(\mu_X\|\pi_k)=o(1).$$ Here $D$ is relative entropy with natural logarithms. More precisely, for each rank of that random tuple, let $a_t$ be the squared conditional next-card mass error averaged over both the input tuple and the shuffle. Then $a_t$ satisfies, for $r\ge1$, $$a_{(r+1)d}\le \kappa_{\delta,d} a_{rd}
       +d n(n+1)e^{-\delta\sqrt n/8},\qquad
 \kappa_{\delta,d}=(1-\delta/4)^{\lfloor d/2\rfloor}
                         +2^{-\lceil d/2\rceil}.
 \label{lifts:cycle-recurrence}$$ Thus one may choose $\alpha>0$ with $\kappa_{\delta,d}\le n^{-\alpha}$ eventually and then any fixed $R$ with $\alpha(R-1)>4$.

For a sufficiently large absolute integer $C_0$, at $t=C_0d$, $$\mathbb P\{\hbox{walker and baseline }j\hbox{ coincide at }t\}
 \le \frac1m+O\bigl((1+t)^3/n^2\bigr),
 \label{eq:c-baseline-coincidence}$$ uniformly over $m\ge\epsilon n$ and $j$.

For all sufficiently large $d$, $$\|\widehat K(\lambda)\|_{\rm op}\le n^{-k/10}
 \quad\hbox{if}\quad 1\le k=n-\lambda_1\le n^{1/20}.
 \label{eq:c-true-sweep-sparse}$$ If $\lambda$ has more than $n/2$ rows, then $\widehat K(\lambda)=0$.
